  "source": "<a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/07141239/TMUA-2022-paper-2.pdf#page=3\">TMUA 2022 · Paper 2 · Question 1</a> · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" href=\"https://uat-wp.s3.eu-west-2.amazonaws.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/06/04105227/TMUA-2022-paper-2-worked-answers.pdf#page=3\">Official worked answer</a>",
  "label": "TMUA 2022 · Paper 2 · Question 1",
  "lead": "<img class=\"source-question\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\" aria-label=\"Enlarge question\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"TMUA 2022 · Paper 2 · Question 1 — Count the stationary points of a quartic polynomial.\">",
  "options": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D",
    "E"
  ],
  "correct": "B",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Translate stationary points into an equation",
      "body": "A stationary point has zero gradient. Differentiate the polynomial term by term before trying to count such points.",
      "recap": "The derivative of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.",
      "pitfall": "The constant term contributes zero; it does not produce another condition.",
      "pause": "Write the complete derivative.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l18",
          "reminder": "Apply the power rule to every nonconstant term."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Keep every factor",
      "body": "Factor the derivative by taking out its common numerical and variable factors.",
      "recap": "A product is zero when at least one factor is zero.",
      "pitfall": "Dividing by a variable factor could discard a stationary point.",
      "pause": "Write the separate equations supplied by your factors.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l19",
          "reminder": "Factor the derivative before analysing its zeros."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check whether the remaining quadratic can vanish",
      "body": "For the quadratic factor, calculate the discriminant or complete the square. Then combine its real solutions with those from the other factor.",
      "recap": "A quadratic has no real roots when its discriminant is negative.",
      "pitfall": "Count distinct real inputs, not the degree of the derivative.",
      "pause": "Determine the quadratic root count and hence the number of stationary points.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l03",
          "reminder": "The discriminant distinguishes zero, one and two real quadratic roots."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Differentiating gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>12</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p><p>The quadratic factor has discriminant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and is never zero for real <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>There is one stationary point. <strong>Answer: B.</strong></p>",
